Telltale's The Walking Dead isn't the  first game to employ the episodic format  nor is it the first video game to have a  wonderfully engaging story but The  Walking Dead engages players in its  narrative in a unique way and telltale  has made interesting and deliberate game  design decisions in order to do so it  basically breaks down into decision  making a balance between scripted and  rewards and sequences as well as the  forging of an emotional connection  between the player and the characters  and circumstances they encounter in game  no spoilers I promise  obviously these three are inexorably  linked but let's try and break it down  first up we've got the main mechanic of  The Walking Dead which permeates  everything from dialogue options to  binary impulse decisions and that's  choice and consequence the  choose-your-own-adventure nature of The  Walking Dead certainly seems to appeal  to some people more than others the  series has even come under fire for  appearing to be more of a movie than a  game due to its focus on often showing  the play of the story rather than having  them directly control the action that  telltale deliberately fashion so  narrative around play a choice although  some decisions are more impactful than  others nearly everything you choose  makes a difference telltale has an  optional feature which tells the player  when they've said or done something that  another character is going to remember  take for example you siding with one  person over another in an argument both  characters will recall the choice you  made and that will inevitably have  repercussions down the track knowing  that your decisions no matter how  trivial have a perceivable effect on the  world and it's characters keeps you more  invested in the protagonist there's a  section where you have a limited amount  of feared to hand out to your companions  if you see the children their parents  will be happy but you have to ignore  some of the other survivors this has the  potential to breed resentment  who misses out on the solely needed  supplies your character's personality is  determined for your actions season  impact upon which characters have your  back when you need it it's important  that these decisions are always on a  timer on the one hand it's the game  reminding you that no one in real life  is going to wait five minutes for you to  continue a conversation but it's also a  reminder that despite how slow things  might be going dire situations are  always looming and inevitable and you  have to keep moving on and then the dire  stuff happens the walking dead hits you  with split second binary decision making  sometimes when you don't expect it  huge choices like deciding who to save  or sacrifice from the zombie driven  peril after learn at you to resolve in  six seconds and these choices often have  the largest consequences consequential  decision-making causes players to  inevitably feel much more responsible  and accountable for their actions no  matter how small and help you think  about how those actions might help hurt  or generally affect the characters who  might be making similar decisions  regarding you later on a hotly debated  feature of The Walking Dead is the  balance between scripted sequences and  branching paths there's been some  grumbling that while there is a lot of  decision-making in The Walking Dead none  of it is meaningful because even the  divergent paths tend towards similar or  sometimes the very same outcome while  the outcry is fair we have to remember  that the Walking Dead ultimately has a  very specific idea of where the plot is  going and where it ends in season 1 the  protagonist has one broad ultimate goal  so telltale isn't exactly going to let  you say screw this and kill yourself for  instance you're also often part of a  larger group so while you may be able to  sway individuals Swain group mentality  is an objectively difficult task in the  scenarios that you're in let's go back  to the idea of the inevitability a  zombie apocalypse brings with it  sometimes you get in a situation where  there's no time to decide and you just  have to keep moving The Walking Dead  knows this and some  keeps pushing you further forward even  if you don't necessarily want to so long  cutscenes happen they usually depict  trouble and QuickTime sequences another  common complaints only happen during  life or death scenarios meaning there's  usually only one way out and you'll be  kept on track at the same time the  interactive freedom you have during  quieter moments is vital to keep players  invested in the scripted sections  it's the unscripted moments which let  the player explore decide and ultimately  feel that they belong to the narrative  world and understand why the  circumstances within it are so important  to the characters people who play The  Walking Dead will experience a lot of  the same major plot points so yeah it's  linear in that sense but the journey to  those points and the circumstances  surrounding how everything happened will  always be unique lasts up and what is  really a culmination of everything else  is the way in which telltale ensures  that players have an emotional  connection to the characters and the  narratives there's a moment in season 2  of The Walking Dead where you finally  have the upper hand over an evil  character who spent all his time  manipulating and killing all the  characters I'm a pretty emotionally  driven person who still cries at the  beginning of Finding Nemo so it wasn't  totally out of character for me to be  dragged into the drama of it all but I  remember being surprised at how angry I  was the individual had murdered  innocence and brutally injured somebody  who I decided through the course of the  game was one of my protagonists closest  friends earlier in the story I'd chosen  to stand up to the villain and I was  defeated I tried and failed to reason  with him when he acted out and now I was  genuinely angry I realized that through  some combination of the way telltale had  crafted this character and the choices I  had personally made in an attempt to  reason with his cruel dictatorship that  I really really hated him I never found  myself hating a fictional character so  incredibly as I did this one and it's to  tell tales credit that they can so often  elicit strong emotional reactions like  mine The Walking Dead isn't everybody's  cup of tea and I get that but the way  they manage to tell the story they  wanted to tell while still making the  player feel like it was their own  feeling involved and personally  accountable is  SIV Telltale's walking-dead formula is  something new and despite its flaws it's  an important point for video games  storytelling let me know down in the  comments if you're a fan of The Walking  Dead or if there's another game you can  think of with an epic story as involved  as this one I'll be back next week with  more things to say to your faces  you
 

